.TH CLONE 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
clone \- create a Linux child with a separate stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sched.h>
.B int clone(int (*function)(void*), void* stack, int flags, void* argument);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a Linux child that invokes function(argument) on the supplied
downward\-growing stack. The function's return value is its exit status.
The low flag bits specify the exit signal, typically SIGCHLD. The stack
must remain mapped until the child exits. Unlike glibc's variadic clone,
Crunch requires exactly four arguments and does not support flags requiring
parent or child TID, pidfd, or TLS arguments.
.PP
CLONE_VM and CLONE_THREAD do not make Crunch thread\-safe: errno, allocator
and other runtime state are process\-wide and unsynchronized. The caller
must coordinate shared resources and signals.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the child ID in the parent, or \-1 with errno set.
Success leaves errno unchanged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fork (3crunch),
.BR clone (2)
